  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Harmonics",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret positive and negative harmonics as paired complex exponentials whose coefficients combine to represent each nonzero harmonic of a periodic signal.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Harmonics",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Positive and Negative Harmonics",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the complex Fourier series by integer harmonic index. $k=0$ is the DC term $C_0$, and every nonzero harmonic is read as a $+k$ / $-k$ pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} C_k e^{jk\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the simplest nonzero harmonic, $k=1$. Read $C_1e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ and $C_{-1}e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$ as one paired contribution for the first harmonic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C_1e^{j\\omega_0 t}+C_{-1}e^{-j\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same pairing rule holds for every $k>0$. The positive and negative indices are two contributions to one nonzero harmonic, not two separate harmonics.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=C_0+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(C_k e^{jk\\omega_0 t}+C_{-k}e^{-jk\\omega_0 t}\\big)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep $C_0$ separate as the unpaired DC term. That cleanly separates the DC component from all nonzero harmonics in later examples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Harmonics",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying Positive and Negative Harmonic Indices",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=C_0+C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t}+C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}$, identify which term is DC, which term is positive index, and which term is negative index.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the signed harmonic index attached to each term. Read the sign on the index to decide whether the term is zero, positive, or negative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=C_0+C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t}+C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "$C_0$ is the zero-index DC term. $C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t}$ is the $+2$ harmonic term, and $C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}$ is the $-2$ harmonic term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C_0 \\rightarrow 0,\\qquad C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t} \\rightarrow +2,\\qquad C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t} \\rightarrow -2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signed indices are $0$, $+2$, and $-2$. The sign on the harmonic index, not the coefficient name alone, is what classifies each term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Harmonics",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Matching the Positive and Negative Terms for the Same Harmonic",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "From the terms $C_1e^{j\\omega_0 t}$, $C_{-1}e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$, $C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t}$, and $C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}$, match the positive and negative terms that belong to the same harmonic.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the absolute harmonic index. Terms match only when the magnitude is the same and the signs are opposite.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C_1e^{j\\omega_0 t},\\qquad C_{-1}e^{-j\\omega_0 t},\\qquad C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t},\\qquad C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match $C_1e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ with $C_{-1}e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$, and match $C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t}$ with $C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}$. Each pair uses the same harmonic number with opposite signs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\big(C_1e^{j\\omega_0 t},\\,C_{-1}e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\big),\\qquad \\big(C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t},\\,C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}\\big)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the answer is two matched harmonic pairs: one pair for harmonic number $1$ and one pair for harmonic number $2$. Nothing else is left unpaired in this example.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Harmonics",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting a Harmonic Pair as One Nonzero Frequency Component",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Interpret the pair $C_4e^{j4\\omega_0 t}+C_{-4}e^{-j4\\omega_0 t}$ as one nonzero harmonic component and state what harmonic number it represents.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the matched $+4$ / $-4$ pair. Read the two terms together as one nonzero harmonic component, not as separate harmonics.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C_4e^{j4\\omega_0 t}+C_{-4}e^{-j4\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the $k=4$ harmonic, so the frequency magnitude is $4\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "k=4,\\qquad \\omega=4\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the pair names one nonzero frequency component at $4\\omega_0$, and the two coefficients together describe that single component.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Positive and Negative Harmonics",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Separating Nonzero Harmonic Pairs from the DC Term",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Separate the DC term from the nonzero harmonic pairs in $x(t)=C_0+C_1e^{j\\omega_0 t}+C_{-1}e^{-j\\omega_0 t}+C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t}+C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the unpaired $C_0$ term. Everything with a $+k$ / $-k$ index belongs to a nonzero harmonic pair, while $C_0$ is the zero-frequency DC term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=C_0+C_1e^{j\\omega_0 t}+C_{-1}e^{-j\\omega_0 t}+C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t}+C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So isolate $C_0$ first, then group the $+1$ and $-1$ terms together and the $+2$ and $-2$ terms together.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=C_0+\\big(C_1e^{j\\omega_0 t}+C_{-1}e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\big)+\\big(C_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t}+C_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}\\big)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That leaves one DC term and two nonzero harmonic pairs, one for $k=1$ and one for $k=2$. The DC term stays separate from every nonzero harmonic pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
